{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779167067,"nonce":"Nr9rsD5fgj6uXJKxEYMgy5eOmLg=","message":"## Implementation spec before editing\n\nThe selector browser should behave like an authoring picker, not a raw state dump. It should help the user choose a valid source/target for objectives and metrics in the same interaction flow where they are editing those terms.\n\nDesign stance from the current code:\n\n- Keep `StudioSelectorRef` as the durable selector record. Do not introduce a parallel frontend-only selector format.\n- Replace the current freeform `Substate / index` field in the objective inspector with a searchable selector chooser that writes back to `source_selector` / `target_selector` through the same objective update path.\n- Keep compact strings such as `path:states.mechanics.effector.pos` as serialization/lowering output, but the UI should show labels, role, shape, dtype, units/frame, and time/index affordances where useful.\n- Build the first slice from static/synthetic introspection derived from existing scenario entities, graph ports, task/mechanics metadata, analysis field paths, and known RLRMP state paths. Leave the API boundary obvious so runtime backend introspection can replace or enrich it later.\n\nInteraction model:\n\n1. In the objective inspector, the source row has a chooser button/search surface. It opens a compact list grouped by intent: graph ports, state paths under the selected port, task objects, mechanics objects, probes, and analysis/artifact fields when present.\n2. Search filters by label/path/namespace. Rows show the minimum useful context: label, compact path, and shape/unit hints. Avoid headings that merely restate the data model.\n3. Choosing a state path updates the objective term immediately and keeps the related graph port metadata so Graph highlighting still works.\n4. Time/index controls stay next to the objective term because selecting a time-varying path is only half the user intent. For this slice, support final/all/range/custom through the existing temporal selector fields and store any path slice/index in selector metadata.\n5. The Objectives projection table should show a readable selector label and a small browser/edit affordance, not raw database keys across multiple columns.\n\nPrecedent patterns to carry forward:\n\n- Unity-style selection and inspector: one selection drives editing; the picker should write back to the selected term rather than sending the user to a disconnected browser.\n- CAD/CAE parameter browsers: favor favorites/relevant parameters, search, units, and editable formula/value fields over enumerating every internal key.\n- IDE symbol pickers: fast search, grouped results, and keyboard-friendly selection matter more than nested tree ornamentation.\n\nAcceptance for this slice:\n\n- Objective source selection can pick graph ports and deeper state paths such as effector position/velocity and network hidden state without typing the path manually.\n- The selected path lowers to the existing loss selector compact string and preserves graph-port relation metadata for highlighting.\n- Tests cover selector option derivation and selector application to objective terms.\n- The UI does not add a separate metadata panel listing all keys just because they exist.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}